tiré de 
LIBRI BOTANICI Vol. 8 A Monograph of Marasmius,Collybia and related genera in Europe
Part 1: Marasmius, Setulipes, and Marasmiellus.
by Vladimfr Antonfn and  Machiel E. Noordeloos

1,2 $ Basidiocarps pleurotoid, stipe very short, eccentric or lateral, reduced and sometimes  almost lacking in mature specimens$ 
1,5 $ Basidiocarps marasmioid or collybioid, stipe normally developed and central in mature  specimens
1,50$ habitats
1,55$ référence
2,0 $ Pileus 5-15 mm, distinctly pigmente d, brown or ochraceous-pink; pileipellis made up  of two types of elements; pileocystidia capitate; on old stems and leaf-sheaths of  Typha latifolia, rarely on Carex. $M. menieri  21.
2,3 $ Pileus 1-5 mm broad, white to pale ochraceous; pileipellis made up of one type of elements;  pileocystidia not capitate; on different substrata$ 
3,0 $ Lamellae absent, hymenium smooth or rarely slightly wrinkled; pileus and stipe with  very slender, tapering cystidia, 60-120 x 3.0-10 zzm; on leaf-sheets of Cladium mariseus $M. cornelii  20.
3,4 $ Lamellae present; pileo- and caulocystidia never tapering into a pointed apex; on sedges  (Carex spp.) in marshes en fens$ 
4,0 $ Pileus white; lamellae fold-like, often furcate; basidia 2-spored; spores (12-)16.5-22.5  (-24.5) x (4.5-)5.0-6.5(-7.0),um $M. caricis  19.
4,0 $ Pileus very pale ochraceous-pink with paler margin; lamellae usually well-developed  and reaching the margin of pileus; basidia 4-spored; spores (11.5-)4.0-18.0(-22.0) x  4.5-6.0( -7.0) ,um $M. pseudocaricis  22.
5,6 $ Lamellae attached to a distinct free collarium round the apex of stipe$ 
5,12 $ Lamellae free or attached to the stipe, non-collariate, rarely partly loosening from the  stipe in form of a pseudocollarium$ 
6,7 $ Pileus bright orange, orange-brown, red, reddish-yellow or red-brown$ 
6,8 $ Pileus white, grey or brownish$ 
7,0 $ Pileus up to 5 mm broad; lamellae distant (L =(4-)6-8, white; spores (9.0-)11-16(-18)  ,um long; pileipellis with broom cells of the Rotalis-type $M. graminum  6.
7,0 $ Pileus 4-10(-15) mm broad; lamellae more crowded (L = 9-13), cream-coloured to  yellowish, rarely purely white; spores (7.5-)8.0-11(-13) ,um long; pileipellis with  broom cells of the Siccus-type $M. curreyi  7.
8,9 $ Only few lamellae present (L = 6-9)$ 
8,10 $ With more lamellae (L > 10)$ 
9,0 $ Basidia 2-spored, short, 17-22,um long; spores 8.0-12(-14) x 4.5-6.0(-7.0) ,um; on  grasses and sedges, in particular on Phragmites, Baldingera and Carex spp. in moist  localities, swamps, etc. $M. limosus  4.
9,0 $ Basidia 4-spored, 30.5-35 ,um long; spores (10-)11.5-19 x 4.0-6.5 zzm; on leaves of  Duschekia alnobetula (= Alnus viridis) $M. alniphilus  5.
10,0 $ Pileus white to cream-coloured when fresh with greyish centre (papilla), 4-14(-18) mm  broad, with 17-21(-25) lamellae; on dead wood (sticks, branches, bark). $M. rotula  1.
10,11 $ Pileus beige-brown when fresh, or when white or whitish, then with distinctly  delimitated, darker, brown or blackish central papilla, usually smaller, pileus never  exceeding 10 mm, with 11-17(-19) lamellae; never on deciduous wood$ 
11,0 $ Pileus sorne shade of brown when fresh; L = 11-15(-16), in average 13-14; projections  of broom cells in pileipellis (1.0-)2.0-4.0( -5.0),um long; stipe sometimes with distinct  side-branches; on leaves of deciduous trees, especially of Quercus and Fagus $M. bulliardii  3.
11,0 $ Pileus white when fresh with grey to grey-brown papilla; L = (12-)13-17(-19), in  average 14-15; projections of broom cells in pileipellis 1.0-2.0(-3.5) ,um long; stipe  never with side-branches; on needles and litter of coniferous trees (Picea, rarely Abies  or Pinus) $M. wettsteinii  2.
12,13 $ Basidiocarps typically marasmioid with membranaceous, usually less than 10 mm  broad pileus; stipe insititious, filiform$ 
12,26 $ Basidiocarps collybioid with more or less fleshy, usually distinctly more than 10 mm  broad pileus; stipe non-insititious, but with basal mycelium, usually not filiform$ 
13,14 $ Pileus white or whitish cream$ 
13,21 $ Pileus distinctly coloured$ 
14,0 $ Pileipellis made up of broom cells; lamellae reduced, usually not reaching the margin  of pileus; spores (11-)14-17 x 3.0-3.5(-4.0) zzm; hyphae of trama non-dextrinoid; on  Hedera helix. $M. epiphylloides  18.
14,15 $ Pileipellis made up of smooth or slightly roughened elements; lamellae reduced or  well-developed; hyphae of trama non-dextrinoid or dextrinoid at least in apex of stipe;  on different substrata$ 
15,16 $ Hyphae of trama dextrinoid, at least in apex of stipe; lamellae well-developed$ 
15,17 $ Hyphae always non-dextrinoid; lamellae usually more or less reduced, less frequently  well-developed$ 
16,0 $ Spores (9.0-)10-14.5 x 4.0-6.0 ,um, usually broadly fusiform; stipe with up to 1 mm  long, hyaline hairs, especially when young; pileus with 5-7(-11) entire lamellae; cheilocystidia  cylindrical to fusiform, often capitate; hyphae in apex of stipe dextrinoid,  in other parts not. $M. setosus  16.
16,0 $ Spores 5.0-7.5 x (2.5-)3.0-4.0 ,um, ellipsoid; stipe without such long hairs; pileus with  up to 16 entire lamellae; cheilocystidia clavate, with one or more apical projections;  hyphae in aIl parts of basidiocarp dextrinoid (except for subhymenium) $M. saccharinus  17.
17,18 $ Pileus small, 1-4 mm broad; lamellae reduced, usually not reaching the margin of the  pileus; spores (7.5-)10-14 x 3.5-4.0,um, almost cylindrical; basidia mostly 2-spored;  hyphae clampless$ 
17,19 $ Pileus up to 10 mm broad; lamellae reduced or well-developed; spores 8.0-12(-14) x  3.0-5.0,um, usually broadly ellipsoid; basidia usually 4-spored; hyphae clamped$M. epiphylloides  18.
18,0 $ Spores (9,5-)10-14 x 3.0-4.5 ,um; Q = 3.1; on leaves of Populus $ 
18,0 $ Spores (7.5-)9.5-12 x 3.0-4.0 zzm; Q = 2.8; on leaves of Sorbus aucuparia $ 
19,0 $ Lamellae strongly reduced ta low veins; basidia 4-, 3- or 2-spored; pi1eipellis made up  of broadly clavate, fusiform or vesiculose, thin-walled elements; on Helleborus eorsieus $ 
19,20 $ Lamellae well-developed or reduced, but always distinct; basidia 4-spored; elements  of pileipellis broadly lageniform or fusiform, thin- or thick-walled; on other substrata$M. setosus  16.
20,0 $ Elements of pileipellis more or less thick-walled; lamellae reduced, usually not reaching the margin of the pileus $M. saccharinus  17.
20,0 $ Elements of pileipellis more or less thin-walled; lamellae well-developed and reaching  the margin of the pileus $M. tenuiparietalis  13.
21,0 $ Pileus and stipe with long, setiform hairs; on dead leaves of llex europaeus $M. hudsonii  10.
21,22 $ Pileus and stipe without setiform hairs; on other substrata$ 
22,23 $ Pileipellis a true hymeniderm, made up of regularly shaped broom cells of the Rotalistype;  pileus usually small, 0.5-7 mm broad, dark brown to red-brown, at least at centre$ 
22,25 $ Pileipellis a true hymeniderm in primordial stages only, later on an irregular trichoderm  of very irregular diverticulate elements; pileus somewhat larger, 3-15 mm broad,  with some shade of brown, flesh-colour, pink or creamy-white$ 
23,0 $ Cheilocystidia of one type: lageniform or tibiiform; pleurocystidia absent; spores (7.0-)  8.0-12.5(-13),um long; on leaves of Buxus $M. buxi  11.
23,24 $ Cheilocystidia of two types: broom cells and fusiform, clavate or lageniform; pleurocystidia  present; spores 6.0-10 ,um long; on other substrata$ 
24,0 $ Pileus distinctly bicoloured: with dark centre and pale marginal zone; L =10-14; spores  8-10 x 3.5-5.5 zzm; pileo- and caulocystidia absent; on leaves of evergreen and deciduous  trees and shrubs in mediterranean areas $M. corbariensis  8.
24,0 $ Pileus uniformly coloured; L = (0-)3-9; spores 6.0-9.5 x 2.0-5.0 ,um; pileo- and caulocystidia  present; on leaves of Populus species $M. minutus  9.
25,0 $ Stipe blackish brown, glabrous, shining; pileus chocolate-brown or dark pinkish brown  with pinkish yellow to pale pink margin; lamellae incarnate to pinkish brown; on        sticks, needles and leaves of coniferous and deciduous trees and shrubs (if smell ofrotten cabbage and stipe hairy, compare Micromphale perforans) $Setulipes androsaceus  43. 
25,0 $ Stipe sordid red-brown, finely hairy, especially when young, glabrescent with age;  pileus pale pink or pinkish brown with pale, almost white margin; lamellae white to  pale cream-coloured; on dead leaves of Quercus $Setulipes quercophilus  44. 
26,27 $ Smel1 strong, like garlic$ 
26,30 $ Smel1 different or absent$ 
27,0 $ Stipe glabrous, polished, shining; cheilocystidia present in form of broom cells $M. scorodonius  33.
27,28 $ Stipe pruinose or hairy; cheilocystidia absent or lageniform, never in form of broom  cells$ 
28,0 $ Stipe blackish brown; lamel1ae often with irregularly serrate edge; pileus grey to dark grey-brown, not translucently striate; cheilocystidia conspicuous, lageniform; on wood  and branches of Fagus, rarely also on the leaves $M. alliaceus  32.
28,29 $ Stipe red-brown; lamellae with entire edge; pileus red-brown or grey-brown, distinctly  translucently striate; cheilo- and pleurocystidia both present or both absent; cheilocystidia,  if present inconspicuous, clavate-cylindrical or fusiform; on other substrate$ 
29,0 $ Lamel1ae distant, L = 12-18; basidia 2-spored; cheilo- and pleurocystidia present; in  arctic snow-beds with Salix herbacea $M. kallioneus  30.
29,0 $ Lamel1ae crowded, L = 19-28; basidia 4-spored; cheilo- and pleurocystidia absent; on  dead leaves of deciduous trees, preferably Quercus in boreal and temperate regions $M. querceus  34.
30,0 $ Lamellae pale yellow to yellow brown with conspicuous brown setiform cystidia on  pileus, lamellae and stipe, (visible with strong lens); piieipellis with broom cells of the  Siccus-type $M. cohaerens  29.
30,31 $ Lamel1ae white or pale, never with setiform cystidia$ 
31,32 $ Stipe glabrous, polished, shining$ 
31,43 $ Stipe pruinose or hairy$ 
32,33 $ Elements of pileipellis smooth$ 
32,36 $ Pileipel1is with broom cells$ 
33,0 $ Stipe white, strongly striate-fissurate; pileus purplish-brown $M. fissipes  39.
33,34 $ Stipe coloured, not distinctly striate; pileus differently coloured$ 
34,0 $ Pileus and stipe about the same pale ochraceous-brown colour; stipe hol1ow; basidiocarps  rather fragile like in most Collybia-species; cheilocystidia absent; in grasslands $M. collinus  38.
34,35 $ Pileus and stipe differently coloured; fruitbodies rather tough; cheilocystidia present;  usual1y in forest$ 
35,0 $ Pileus colour ranging from white to grey with violaceous tinge; stipe white at first  then reddening from base upwards, final1y red-brown; cheilocystidia irregularly c1avate;  smell often like almonds $M. wynnei  37.
35,0 $ Pileus pale brown; stipe red-brown with darker base; cheilocystidia in form of broom  cells; smell none $M. scorodonius (odourless form)  33.
36,37 $ Species so far only found in hot greenhouses, and probably of tropical origin$ 
36,39 $ Species found in natural habitats$ 
37,0 $ Pileus pale grey-brown with flesh-coloured centre; lamellae distant, L = 12-13; cheilocystidia of two-types: Siccus-type broom cells mixed with smooth c1avate to lecithiform elements . $M. anisocystidiatus  
37,38 $ Pileus dark brown; lamellae more crowded, L = about 15-20; cheilocystidia of the Siceus- type only$ 
38,0 $ Pileus and stipe reddish-brown; spores (12-)13-19 x 4.0-5.0 ,um, Q =3.3; broom cells of pileipellis with 2.0-8.0 ,um long projections $M. skalae  26.
38,0 $ Pileus and stipe dull brown without reddish tinge, rarely with reddish tinge when old; spores 18-22 x 3.0-5.0 ,um, Q =4.9; broom cells of pileipellis with up to 1O(-15),um long projections $M. teplicensis  28.
39,0 $ Stipe, lamellae, and pileus with brown, thick-walled cystidia $M. cohaerens  29.
39,40 $ Stipe and pileus without such thick-walled cystidia$ 
40,0 $ Stipe citrine-yellow in apical part, turning green on drying $M. ventalloi  25.
40,41 $ Stipe pale yellow at apex, not turning green$ 
41,0 $ Pileus up to 30 mm broad, usually bright orange-yellow when fresh; lamellae narrow, often somewhat reduced and vein-like; in litter in bore al forest and heath-like vegetation $M. siccus  23.
41,42 $ Pileus up to 15 mm broad, dull yellowish-ochre or yellowish white when fresh; lamellae well-developed, often ventricose; in grassland and on remnants of grasses in rather exposed, often xerophytic grassland communities$ 
42,0 $ Spores (12-)16-22(-24) x (3.0-)3.5-4.5(-6.0) ,um, Q = 4.4 $M. anomalus var. anomalus  24a.
42,0 $ Spores 12-18(-19) x (3.5-)4.0-5.0(-6.0) ,um, Q = 3.8 $M. anomalus var. microsporus  24b.
43,44 $ Hyphae of context non-dextrinoid$ 
43,45 $ Hyphae of context dextrinoid$ 
44,0 $ Stipe long and slender, 60-150 x 1.5-2.5 mm; grey-brown with white pruinose covering when mature; spores broadly fusiform to amygdaliform; pileo- and caulocystidia thin-walled, hyaline or slightly pigmented; in forest on or near rhizomes of Pteridium aquilinum $M. undatus  31.
44,0 $ Stipe short, 5-40(-60) x (0.5-)1-2 mm, dark brown with rusty brown covering; spores ellipsoid or amygdaliform; pileo- and caulocystidia brown, thick-walled; on old, dead parts of Dryas spp. in alpine and arctic habitats $M. epidryas  35.
45,0 $ Thick-walled setiform cystidia present on pileus and stipe; cheilo- and pleurocystidia present $M. torquescens  30.
45,46 $ Thick-walled setiform cystidia absent; cheilocystidia present or absent; pleurocystidia always absent$ 
46,0 $ Pileus (and stipe) red-brown; spores 9.5-12 x 5.0-6.5 ,um, amygdaliform $M. todeae s. Bresinsky  41.
46,47 $ Pileus differently coloured; spores smaller and differently shaped$ 
47,0 $ Pileus pale ochraceous, cream-coloured, strongly pallescent to almost white; stipe concolorous with pileus, tomentose, rather elastic; spores (7.0-)8.0-10.5(-11.5) x 4.0-6.0 ,um; cheilocystidia absent $M. oreades  40.
47,48 $ Pileus and stipe differently coloured; cheilocystidia present; spores smaller$ 
48,0 $ Pileus white or grey, often with violaceous tinge; stipe white at first, soon becoming reddish to orange-brown from base upwards, finally reddish brown, squamulose; pileocystidia absent; cheilocystidia irregularly clavate to coralloid; elements of pileipellis smooth $M. wynnei  37.
48,0 $ Pileus dirty grey or blackish with ochraceous-tinged centre; stipe white at apex, rusty brown to black towards base, pruinose above, woolly-tomentose below; pileocystidia present; cheilocystidia mostly fusiform or sublageniform, elements of pileipellis smooth and of broom cells type $M. cespitum  42.

